From 68001de449b19bb5a18ccc0b704065c615d35e39 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ben Vanik <ben.vanik@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:46:32 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [HAL] Fail impossible AMDGPU completion epochs (#1627)

AMDGPU notification retirement used to clamp a hardware completion epoch
to the last host-published epoch and discard the original relationship.
A captured shutdown had submitted, published, and drained epoch 143582
while hardware reported 143583. The epoch wait therefore remained ready,
repeatedly won over an already-raised stop signal, and left device
teardown joined to a completion thread that could never make progress.

### Completion contract

Hardware is allowed to finish an assembled packet before its submitting
thread publishes the final host resource accesses. The notification
drain now returns the completed and published epochs acquired by its
existing signal and atomic loads. When completion is not ahead, the
common path performs no additional query and takes no new lock.

When completion is ahead, the host queue releases the completion-drain
mutex and acquires the submission mutex. This waits out the sole
legitimate publisher without introducing the inverse lock order that
completion callbacks would deadlock against. It then acquires both
epochs again:

- If publication caught up, the observation was the valid
fast-completion race and retirement continues normally.
- If hardware remains ahead, no admitted host submission owns that
completion. The queue records a terminal INTERNAL failure instead of
normalizing the counter.

### Failure behavior

A persistent mismatch publishes an attributed DRIVER_FAILURE event
containing the completed, published, submitted, and drained epochs. The
same full status becomes sticky on the queue and logical device, and the
stop signal wakes the completion service so submitted and deferred
operations take the existing terminal failure path.

The extra completion remains a hard invariant failure. This change does
not prefer the stop signal, forgive the decrement, or retire metadata
that the host never published.

### Coverage

The hardware-backed lifecycle coverage models both sides of the
boundary. One test lets hardware complete while a real publisher
continues to own the submission mutex and proves that semaphore
publication waits for host retirement without failing the queue. A
second starts from a normally submitted and drained epoch, advances only
the hardware signal by one, and proves that the queue reports the exact
failure, latches device failure, and exits cleanly.

The notification-ring coverage also checks that the drain exposes the
same completed and published epochs used for retirement decisions.
